---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

// DRAFT. The ladder itself is settled — see docs/roadmaps/website.md. Only the
// last rung is honestly "coming soon"; the first one works right now.

const readQuery = `curl -X POST https://sempods.org/aaltra/_system/sparql/query \\
  -H "Content-Type: application/sparql-query" \\
  -H "Accept: application/sparql-results+json" \\
  --data 'PREFIX schema: <https://schema.org/>
SELECT ?e ?name ?start WHERE {
  ?e a schema:Event ; schema:name ?name ; schema:startDate ?start
} ORDER BY ?start LIMIT 3'`;

const deps = `implementation(platform("org.sempods:sempods-bom:0.1.0"))
implementation("org.sempods:sempods-client")`;
---

<Base
  title="Start — sempods"
  description="Four ways into sempods, from a single curl against a live pod to building against the published artifacts."
  schemaType="TechArticle"
>
  <h1>Start</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    Four rungs, in order of effort. The first one needs nothing installed and is
    the fastest way to find out whether this is interesting.
  </p>

  <h2>1. Read a live pod</h2>

  <p>
    Public contexts are readable without authentication. This is a real venue's
    calendar, served from its own pod:
  </p>

  <pre><code>{readQuery}</code></pre>

  <p>
    Change <code>aaltra</code> to another pod name and the same request works.
    That is the whole discovery mechanism — there is no registry to consult.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Run your own pod</h2>

  <p>
    <code>deployments/local/compose.yaml</code> in the reference implementation
    brings up a pod, an identity service and the stores behind them. It is one
    command, and it gives you something to write to rather than only read from.
  </p>

  <p>
    Being honest about this rung: once it is running, the interesting part —
    putting your own data in and getting it back out through an app — is the next
    rung, not this one.
  </p>

  <h2>3. Build against it</h2>

  <p>
    The artifacts are on Maven Central, so the starting point is two lines:
  </p>

  <pre><code>{deps}</code></pre>

  <p>
    Java 21 bytecode, so nothing forces you onto a newer toolchain.
    <code>SempodsPodClient</code> covers reading, writing, contexts and SPARQL;
    the token exchange is one call.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Use the apps — not yet</h2>

  <p>
    The applications built on sempods — a task manager, a chat client, an explorer
    — are not open source yet. When they are, this rung becomes the shortest one:
    point an app at a pod and use it. Until then it is honest to say it is not
    available rather than to describe it.
  </p>

  <hr class="divider" />

  <p>
    Stuck, or something did not behave as written? That is worth an
    <a href="https://github.com/sempods/sempods-kotlin/issues">issue</a> — a
    first-run problem is the most valuable bug report a project this young can get.
  </p>
</Base>
